ne -10 && ls && grep -rIl --exclude-dir=.git --exclude-dir=node_modules -i \"create index\\|create table\" . | head"]], "final_message": "The likely culprit is `lower(u.email)` in the `WHERE` clause. I only have the query, not the schema, plans or data sizes, so this is an educated guess.\n\n**Why it's slow**\n- **`lower(u.email) = '...'` can't use a plain index on `users.email`.** The database has to scan every row in `users`, lowercase each email, and compare. That cost grows with the table, so it can look fine for months and then fall over once `users` passes a size threshold or the cache stops fitting. That fits \"since last week\".\n- **`orders.user_id` may not be indexed.** The join needs that index to be cheap. Without it, each matching user means a scan of `orders`.\n- **`SELECT *` pulls every column from both tables.** That includes wide ones like blobs, JSON and address fields. It also yields duplicate column names (`id`, `created_at`), which some ORMs handle badly. It's a secondary cost, not the main one.\n\n**Fixes, in order of payoff**\n1. Add an expression index that matches the predicate. This is Postgres syntax:\n   ```sql\n   CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_users_email_lower ON users (lower(email));\n   ```\n   Alternatively, store emails normalized (or use `citext`) and compare `u.email = lower($1)` against a plain index. That's cleaner long term.\n2. Confirm that `orders(user_id)` has an index, and add one if not.\n3. Replace `SELECT *` with the columns the page actually renders. Also parameterize the email rather than inlining it.\n\n**To confirm before changing anything**, run this on the real data:\n```sql\nEXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS) SELECT ... ;\n```\nIf the plan shows a `Seq Scan on users` with a filter on `lower(email)`, that's it. If `users` is cheap and the time goes into `orders`, it's the missing `user_id` index instead.\n\nI'd also check what changed last week: a deploy that introduced the `lower()`, a data import, or a dropped or invalidated index. `app.log` and `commits.txt` here might show that, and I can look through them if you like. If you can share the schema or the `EXPLAIN` output, I can be more precise.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}